    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Duchy of Aquitaine emerged from the break-up of Carolingian authority in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> had created a sub-kingdom of Aquitaine for his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> in 781, but by the late ninth century real power there had passed to great regional nobles. In 893 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-duke-of-aquitaine">William the Pious</a>, count of Auvergne, began to style himself duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitanians</a>, the first prince to hold the title in this new sense, and in 910 he founded the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Cluny</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, whose monastic reform would spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a>. After the death of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acfred-duke-of-aquitaine">Acfred</a> in 927 the Auvergnat line died out, and the ducal title was disputed between the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and the counts of Poitou. The Poitevin house, often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnulfids">Ramnulfids</a>, prevailed in the mid-tenth century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-duke-of-aquitaine">William III Towhead</a> and held the duchy for nearly two centuries, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>. William V the Great, who ruled from the 990s until 1030, made the dukes among the most respected princes of the kingdom, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-viii-duke-of-aquitaine">Guy-Geoffrey</a>, who reigned as William VIII, acquired the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> in the 1050s and 1060s, extending ducal lordship from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. Aquitaine thus became the largest principality within the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, nominally subject to the king but in practice autonomous.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-hilaire-le-grand"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-hilaire-le-grand.jpg" alt="Saint-Hilaire-le-Grand" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-hilaire-le-grand">Saint-Hilaire-le-Grand</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mairie_st-Hilaire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Garitan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aquitaine was a large and varied land, from the plains of Poitou and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a> to the vineyards of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordelais">Bordelais</a>, the forested Limousin, the uplands of Auvergne and the Gascon country towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. Most people were peasants growing grain and vines under lay and ecclesiastical lords, and the region was dense with monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-martial-limoges">Saint-Martial of Limoges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-hilaire-le-grand">Saint-Hilaire of Poitiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-jean-dangely">Saint-Jean-d'Angély</a>. Salt from the coastal marshes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a> and wine shipped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> were exported to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After 1152, and especially once Poitou was lost to the French crown, Gascon wine found a protected market in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>; in the best years of the early fourteenth century Bordeaux shipped close to 100,000 tuns a year, and English cloth, grain and fish came back in return. Many new fortified towns, the <em>bastides</em>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libourne">Libourne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monpazier">Monpazier</a>, were founded by English and French lords in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries to settle the countryside and secure revenues. The pilgrimage roads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> crossed the duchy, bringing pilgrims and wealth to great Romanesque churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-la-grande">Notre-Dame-la-Grande</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, while the Peace of God movement, launched at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-charroux">Council of Charroux</a> in 989, sought to protect clergy and peasants from aristocratic violence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-taillebourg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-taillebourg.jpg" alt="Battle of Taillebourg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-taillebourg">Battle of Taillebourg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis9_vs_Henri3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chroniques de Saint-Denis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dukes commanded the service of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, the counts and viscounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, Périgord, La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> and the lords of the castles that multiplied across the region from the late tenth century, but these vassals were often in revolt, and much ducal warfare was spent subduing them. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-elector-of-hesse">William IX</a> led a large army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1101, which was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Under the Angevin kings the duchy was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of constant rebellion: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard the Lionheart</a>, invested as duke in 1172, spent years besieging the castles of rebellious barons and was fatally wounded at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-de-chalus-chabrol">Châlus-Chabrol</a> in the Limousin in 1199. The French crown conquered Poitou between 1204 and 1224, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france">Louis VIII</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a>'s attempt to recover it failed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-taillebourg">Battle of Taillebourg</a> in 1242. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, defended by castles and fortified towns and supplied by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, became a frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward the Black Prince</a> marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> on the campaign that ended with his victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poitiers-1356">Battle of Poitiers</a> in 1356, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was captured. English rule ended after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon">Battle of Castillon</a> in July 1453, where French artillery broke the English attack and the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-talbot-1st-earl-of-shrewsbury">John Talbot</a>, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, was killed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis VII of France" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Natalis de Wailly (1805-1886), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ducal government in Aquitaine was always a matter of lordship over powerful men rather than of direct administration. The dukes held Poitou and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> directly, ruled the rest through the homage of counts and viscounts, and depended on alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbeys. The office was hereditary in the Poitevin house, and the dukes did homage to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, though in the tenth and eleventh centuries this was often nominal. William V the Great corresponded with rulers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and was offered the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for himself or his son around 1024–1025, an offer he eventually declined. When Eleanor, heiress of William X, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII</a> in 1137 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry Plantagenet</a> in 1152, the duchy passed into the hands of the kings of France and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, who governed through seneschals. After 1259 the English kings ruled Gascony as dukes of Aquitaine through a seneschal and the constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, who kept the accounts, while the towns, Bordeaux and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> above all, enjoyed wide privileges. Because the English king owed homage to the French king for the duchy, Gascon subjects could appeal against ducal officials to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>, a legal lever the French crown used repeatedly. From 1362 to 1372 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> ruled an enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-aquitaine">Principality of Aquitaine</a> held in full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bordeaux.jpg" alt="Bordeaux" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bordeaux_Place_de_la_Bourse_de_nuit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mariette, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aquitaine's most lasting contribution was cultural. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-elector-of-hesse">William IX</a> (1071–1126) is the earliest troubadour whose songs survive, eleven poems in Occitan that range from the bawdy to the courtly, and the courts of his son William X and his granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> were associated with poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcabru">Marcabru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cercamon">Cercamon</a>. The lyric of <em>fin'amor</em> that the troubadours developed spread to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and shaped European love poetry, from the trouvères and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesänger</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Cluny</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-duke-of-aquitaine">William the Pious</a>, the duchy contributed to the reform of Western monasticism, and through the councils of the Peace of God to the first organised efforts to limit aristocratic warfare. Eleanor herself, queen first of France and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and John, was one of the most powerful women of the medieval West. Politically, the duchy's union with the English crown made it a core of the Angevin Empire and later the principal cause of conflict between England and France: the confiscation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> by Philip VI in 1337 opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. Three centuries of English rule left close commercial ties between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which survive in the British taste for claret.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon.jpg" alt="Battle of Castillon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon">Battle of Castillon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fran%C3%A7ais_5054,_fol._229v,_Bataille_de_Castillon_1453_-_d%C3%A9tail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aquitaine's decline as a great principality began with its absorption into larger dynastic empires. After Eleanor's death in 1204 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II Augustus</a> overran Normandy and Anjou, and the French crown took Poitou in stages, completing the conquest with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> in 1224. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1259">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1259 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> gave up his claims to Normandy, Anjou and Poitou and did homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria">Louis IX</a> for a duchy reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, or Guyenne, with promises of lands in the Limousin, Périgord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quercy">Quercy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agenais">Agenais</a>. The arrangement proved unstable: French kings confiscated the duchy in 1294 and 1324, and Philip VI's confiscation in 1337 started the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. English victories restored a large Aquitaine under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> in 1360, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>'s heavy hearth tax led Gascon lords, among them the count of Armagnac, to appeal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1368, and French armies reconquered most of the principality within a decade. By the late 1370s English rule was confined to a coastal strip around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1435">Treaty of Arras</a> of 1435 the French recovered steadily: Bordeaux surrendered in 1451, rose in 1452 to welcome <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-talbot-1st-earl-of-shrewsbury">John Talbot</a>, and capitulated for good in October 1453 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon">Castillon</a>, when Guyenne was annexed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of Aquitaine matters as the largest principality of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the birthplace of troubadour poetry and the inheritance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>, whose marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry Plantagenet</a> in 1152 made it a core of the Angevin Empire and, through the Gascon question, a central cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>.</em></p>

